-# External Resource Ownership
-
-Mandatory. Applies whenever an agent creates any resource that outlives a
-single tool call and lives outside the git working tree it is editing.
-
-## 1. What Counts As An Owned External Resource
-
-An owned external resource is anything an agent starts, boots, mounts, opens,
-or binds that keeps existing and keeps consuming host or network capacity
-after the command that created it returns. This includes, at minimum:
-
-* a VM (`limactl`, `multipass`, cloud instance, etc.)
-* a container (`docker run`, `podman run`, compose stacks)
-* an emulator or simulator (`simctl boot`, Android emulator, etc.)
-* a mounted disk image (`hdiutil attach`, loopback mounts)
-* a tunnel (`ssh -L`/`-R`, `ngrok`, Tailscale funnels)
-* a background service or long-lived process (dev server, watcher, daemon)
-* remote-host state created for the task (files, directories, git refs, run
-  roots on a remote machine per `INSTRUCTIONS_REMOTE_AGENTS.md`)
-* a listening port bound by the agent's own process
-
-If a command leaves something alive after it exits, it created an owned
-external resource, whether or not that was the point of the command.
-
-## 2. Ownership Is An Obligation, Not A Permission
-
-The agent that creates an owned external resource owns tearing it down, and
-owes that teardown **before handing off** — before ending the turn, closing
-the task, or reporting the work as complete — not "eventually," not "in a
-follow-up," and not "when someone notices." A resource an agent forgets to
-tear down is not somebody else's problem to discover later; it is this
-agent's unfinished task.
-
-## 3. Task-Scoped Naming Is Mandatory, Because A Name Nothing Reads Helps Nobody
-
-Every owned external resource must be named with its task ID
-(`task-<TASK-ID>`, `<TASK-ID>-worktree`, etc.) so it can be attributed if it
-survives. Naming alone does not satisfy this contract: an incident VM was
-already named `task-260819-1e1387` and it still went undetected for weeks,
-because nothing ever read the name back. A name is only useful paired with
-the ledger in §4 and something that actually reads it — today that is a
-human or the agent itself doing the independent verification in §5; see §4
-for the enumeration tooling planned to read this ledger automatically. Name
-every resource, but do not treat naming as the control; the ledger and
-verified teardown are the control.
-
-## 4. Declare Before You Create
-
-Before creating an owned external resource, append a declaration to a
-task-scoped ledger at:
-
-```
-.temp/<TASK-ID>/resources.ndjson
-```
-
-Write the declaration line **before** running the create command, not after
-it succeeds. A crashed or killed run must still leave a trace of what it was
-about to start; a ledger written only on success proves nothing when the
-process never reaches "success."
-
-Each ledger line is one JSON object (newline-delimited JSON), minimally:
-
-```json
-{"ts":"2026-09-03T12:00:00Z","task_id":"TASK-260903-1ve4cu","kind":"vm","name":"task-260903-1ve4cu-vm","state":"declared","create_cmd":"limactl start ..."}
-```
-
-On successful creation, append a second line updating `state` to `"created"`
-(carry the same `name`/`kind`/`task_id` so records join). On teardown, append
-a third line with `state":"torn_down"` only after the independent verification
-in §5 passes; if verification fails, append `state":"teardown_failed"` instead
-and do not report the resource as gone.
-
-Required fields per line: `ts`, `task_id`, `kind`, `name`, `state`
-(`declared` | `created` | `torn_down` | `teardown_failed`). `create_cmd` is
-required on `declared` lines; a `verify_cmd`/`verify_result` pair is required
-on `torn_down` and `teardown_failed` lines.
-
-No automated reader of this ledger exists today. Right now it is read by
-humans and by the agent itself, and "verification" means the independent
-read described in §5 — nothing currently reconciles the ledger against live
-resources for you. The format above is a pinned contract for planned
-consumers that will do that reconciliation automatically:
-
-* an `agents-infra resources` enumeration/reap command —
-  relux-agents-infra `TASK-260903-2mfpon`
-* a board `resource_ack` done-gate that blocks handoff on an unresolved
-  ledger line — skill-project-management `TASK-260903-a8lcn5`
-
-Until those land, an undeclared or unverified resource is caught only by
-whoever actually reads the ledger and runs the checks in §5 — not by
-tooling.
-
-## 5. Teardown Truth: Verify Absence, Never Trust The Delete Command's Exit Code
-
-Running the delete/stop/unmount command is not teardown. Teardown is
-confirming, by an independent read, that the resource is actually gone.
-
-This is not a hypothetical caution. In this project's own history,
-`limactl delete` failed with exit code 99 and the calling code still reported
-`status: ok, residualResources: 0` — the aggregate success path trusted the
-delete command instead of checking what was actually left running. The
-resource stayed alive under a success report until it was found by hand.
-
-The rule this defect forces:
-
-* After running a teardown command, re-list or re-query the resource by an
-  independent read (`limactl list`, `docker ps`, `simctl list devices`,
-  `hdiutil info`, a port scan, a remote `ls`/`test -e`) — never by re-checking
-  the teardown command's own exit code or stdout.
-* Report per-resource outcome, not a single aggregate "ok." A batch teardown
-  where one of three resources fails to disappear is a partial failure, not a
-  success.
-* An unreadable or failed verification read is not evidence of absence.
-  Absence and "I could not check" are different facts — report the latter as
-  unknown/blocked, never silently as torn down.
-
-## 6. No Handoff While An Undeclared Owned Resource Is Live
-
-An agent must not hand off a task — change board status, end a spawned run,
-report completion — while any owned external resource it created is still
-running and either (a) undeclared in the task's `resources.ndjson`, or
-(b) declared but not yet verified torn down. Discovering a leftover resource
-at handoff time means finishing the teardown and verification first, not
-noting it as a follow-up.
-
-See also:
-
-* `INSTRUCTIONS_REMOTE_AGENTS.md` Cleanup.
-* `INSTRUCTIONS_TOOLS.md` Resource Release Check — the before-handoff check
-  that this contract's teardown-verification rules feed.
-* `INSTRUCTIONS_TESTING.md` Ephemeral Test Infrastructure Teardown — the same
-  ownership and verification obligation applied to VMs, emulators, simulators,
-  containers, and test servers created to run a test.
